---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import MustBeTrue from '../components/MustBeTrue.svelte';
---

<Base title="Quote form guide | Fog Off">
  <article class="wrap" style="padding-block:56px 0; max-width:760px;">
    <span class="pill">Guide</span>
    <h1 style="font-size:clamp(2rem,5vw,3rem); letter-spacing:-0.03em; line-height:1.1; margin:16px 0;">
      Getting a fair car insurance quote
    </h1>
    <p style="color:var(--muted); font-size:1.1rem;">
      Short, plain notes on the questions that move your price. This is information, not personal advice.
    </p>

    <h2 class="h2">1. Additional drivers</h2>
    <p>
      In one controlled test, adding a parent who really drove the car cut the quote by about 15%. Adding both parents cut it by about 22%. In a small early survey, it was the change people named most often.
    </p>
    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Fronting warning.</strong> List a person only if they will genuinely drive the car. If a young driver is the main driver, do not name an older relative as the main driver to get a lower price. That is called fronting. It can void your policy.
    </div>

    <h2 class="h2">2. Timing</h2>
    <p>
      Prices often look best about 22 to 26 days before the policy starts. Buying in the last week is often dearer. Set a reminder about 4 weeks before renewal.
    </p>

    <h2 class="h2">3. Black box (telematics)</h2>
    <p>
      A black box gave the biggest drop in our early test, about 38% for a new young driver. It also records how you drive, and poor scores can raise your price. Compare both, and read the terms.
    </p>

    <h2 class="h2">4. Things that changed little</h2>
    <p>
      Small changes to vehicle value, a tracker, or course type moved the price by less than 1% in the test. Differences under about 5% are often noise, because the cheapest insurer can change between runs.
    </p>

    <h2 class="h2">5. Third-party only is not always cheaper</h2>
    <p>
      In the test, third-party-only cover was dearer than comprehensive. Always get a comprehensive quote too.
    </p>

    <h2 class="h2" id="score">How the Fog Off score works</h2>
    <p>
      Every suggestion gets a score from 0 to 100. It shows how much that change is <em>likely to matter for profiles like yours</em>, from limited data. It is a general indicator, not a promise and not a recommendation to you.
    </p>
    <p><strong>Score = Effect × Fit × Evidence</strong>, each taken as a percentage.</p>
    <div class="tbl">
      <div><strong>Effect</strong><span>How much the change moved prices in our tests. A measured change of 40% or more counts as 100. Where we have measured nothing yet, we use a stated assumption: large 70, timing 40, comparing sites 35, other moderate effects 30.</span></div>
      <div><strong>Fit</strong><span>How well the change matches your answers. For example a black box fits a young driver at 100 and an experienced one at 20, and a higher excess fits someone who feels a claim is unlikely better than someone who feels one is likely.</span></div>
      <div><strong>Evidence</strong><span>How much we trust the numbers: community reports 90, strong well-known rules 80, widely repeated advice 70, guidance 60, our small early test 50, one-off or anecdotal 35, mixed results 30.</span></div>
    </div>
    <p>
      <strong>Worked example.</strong> For a young driver, a black box measured about 38% cheaper in our early test. Effect 95, Fit 100, Evidence 50, so 95 × 100% × 50% = <strong>47</strong>, which is High.
      For the job title, the effect is assumed large (70) but the evidence is weak (35) and the fit is 80, so 70 × 80% × 35% ≈ <strong>20</strong>, which only just reaches High.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Bands.</strong> High is 20 or more, Medium is 8 to 19, Low is under 8, using the whole-number score you see. The list is in score order, so the bands and the order always agree.
      Because the three parts are multiplied, one weak part pulls the whole score down. That is deliberate: a change with a big possible effect but poor evidence should not outrank a solid, well-fitting one.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Limits.</strong> The numbers come from one small early test, a short survey and public guidance. They will change as community reports arrive, and the weights themselves are open to challenge. Your own results in the test plan always matter more than any score here.
    </p>

    <h2 class="h2">Answers that must be true when you buy</h2>
    <p>
      It is fine to test changes while you learn how the price works. The quote you <em>buy</em> must use your real details. Tick each item as you check it.
    </p>
    <MustBeTrue client:load />
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .h2 { font-size: 1.45rem; letter-spacing: -0.02em; margin: 44px 0 10px; }
  p { margin: 0 0 14px; }
  .tbl { display: grid; gap: 10px; margin: 10px 0 14px; }
  .tbl div { display: grid; gap: 2px; background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 12px; padding: 12px 14px; }
  .tbl span { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.92rem; }
  .callout {
    background: var(--warn-soft);
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--warn) 35%, transparent);
    color: var(--text);
    padding: 16px 18px;
    border-radius: 14px;
    margin: 18px 0;
  }
</style>
